Message-ID: <50nrhJAU1kQ.5Ti94isFNbgJ@rqtl-disc.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <50nrhJAU1kQ.J62Wqj7QPqwJ@rqtl-disc.groups.google.com>
References: <50nrhJAU1kQ.BoTzTbVNmEUJ@rqtl-disc.groups.google.com> <50nrhJAU1kQ.J62Wqj7QPqwJ@rqtl-disc.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/rqtl-disc/c/50nrhJAU1kQ/m/5Ti94isFNbgJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

You can get artifacts in the fit of tightly linked QTL, especially if there are phenotype outliers. This may be going on with your data.  Ideally, you want at least two markers between any two QTL, but I think even then you can get artifacts.

Also, the calculation of percent variance explained in fitqtl seems to not be appropriate for linked QTL.

karl

